\title{Discrete structures and induced physics}
\tag{notes}
\tag{math}
\tag{draft}
\tag{agent-draft}
\tag{fdip}
\tag{discrete-physics}
\tag{lattice}
\tag{induced-gravity}
\author{utensil}
\date{2026-10-16}

\p{Physics often models length, time and field values as continuous
(infinitely divisible) quantities. Measurements have finite resolution,
so they do not establish that this continuum is fundamental. The real
numbers are its usual mathematical form. These notes start from finite
carriers and exact algebra, asking whether geometry, time and matter can
be induced, with continuum physics recovered at large scales. These are
aims to test. A classical field assigns values to points of space; in this discrete setting,
the points are vertices or edges of a finite graph. Finitely many coordinates need
not mean finitely many possible values: later Gaussian fields explicitly
take real values. The method is to ask what the structure induces and
what must be supplied. The [finite incidence presentation](fdip-0002)
specifies differences along edges; the [alternating four-cycle](fdip-0003)
makes the additional weights explicit. Reordering vertices changes a
representation; changing weights changes the model. Here
[Gaussian elimination](fdip-0018) uses a specified quadratic action
and reference measure.}

\p{Induced geometry provides one motivation. Shamir's dynamical random
spatial lattice yields an effective metric conditional on the existence
of suitable localized low-energy states
\citet{Abstract}{shamir1994dynamical}. This motivates separating supplied
microscopic data, state assumptions and induced structure. The broader
[conjecture](fdip-003X) asks for an arithmetic scheme with a Clifford
sheaf and finite-group action, a moduli-controlled continuum and background
comparison, and quantum-field-theory parameters given by special L-values.
These are targets:
the finite constructions do not derive spacetime, physical parameters,
Standard Model equations or gravitational equations.}

\p{Fields and operator products lead through pairings, parity and
differences to elimination over a ring. States and reference measures
then make real Gaussian expectations, covariance and constrained laws
precise. Gaussian elimination and formal interactions give retained
observables; symmetry, sectors and Clifford constructions lead to spatial
Dirac operators and background comparisons before the arithmetic examples.
A supplied circle gives a fixed-mode continuum comparison; geometric
realization then tests what survives when weights are forgotten. The closing
[synthesis](fdip-003W) relates these results to the conjecture and its
remaining interfaces. The four-cycle recurs throughout, connecting each
new structure to specified fields, weights and observables.}

